meta {
  name: Draft a copy for next session
  type: http
  seq: 28
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/curricula
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Mathematics Class 6 -- draft revision",
    "code": "MATH-6-2025-DRAFT",
    "academic_session_id": "{{sessionId}}",
    "subject_id": "{{subjectId}}",
    "grade_level_id": "{{gradeLevelId}}",
    "board": "CBSE",
    "copied_from_id": "{{curriculumId}}",
    "status": "draft"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.status: eq draft
  res.body.copied_from_id: eq {{curriculumId}}
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("curriculumCopyId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  How a syllabus is revised: copy it, edit the copy, activate the copy and
  archive the original. copied_from_id records where it came from, and
  supersedes_id records what it replaces -- separate fields, because a copy
  used as a starting point is not the same claim as a replacement.

  Both are checked against what the caller may read, and neither may name the
  curriculum itself. A lineage pointing at a syllabus the caller cannot see
  would let them confirm it exists, and a self-reference would make the chain
  a loop that no reader could walk to the end of.

  Note this one is draft. Two active syllabuses for the same class is the 409
  in request 21, and a copy is exactly how somebody would trip over it.
}
